# Normalise line endings.
#
# Without this, a Windows clone with the default `core.autocrlf=true` checks
# every text file out as CRLF. That is not cosmetic here:
#   - install.sh and the test/docker shell scripts are executed by bash, which
#     reads a trailing CR as part of the command and fails in ways that read as
#     "command not found";
#   - fixtures and docs are parsed by `\n`-anchored regexes (the README
#     quick-start contract among them), which silently stop matching;
#   - checksums over text fixtures differ from the Linux CI values.
#
# LF in the working tree on every platform keeps the repo byte-identical to what
# CI and the release pipeline see. Windows editors and PowerShell both handle LF
# fine; the only files that genuinely need CRLF are marked below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the exception: PowerShell tolerates LF, but .bat/.cmd
# dispatch is historically CRLF-sensitive, so keep those native.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.wasm binary
*.sqlite binary
*.exe binary
*.tgz binary
*.gz binary
